{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 903 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "082536ffa32bf5fb5b393dfd7ef1e5f9a665ad0e72489ed9118435b73dead4b3" :edb/db-before-t 902 :edb/db-after-t 903 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534215 50 #inst "2026-10-11T17:52:16.316Z" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171718 1071 "Do the sets below have the same cardinality, and why?\n\n$$\nA=\\{ 4n \\mid \\: n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\}, \\qquad B=\\{ 2, 4,  \\ldots, 20 \\}\n$$" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171718 1071 "Do the sets below have the same cardinality, and why?\n$A=\\{4n \\mid \\: n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\}, \\qquad B=\\{2, 4, \\ldots, 20 \\}$" 13194139534215 17592186045417 false] [17592186171718 1074 "The cardinalities of two sets cannot be equal if one set is finite and the other is infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, notice that\n\n- $A=\\{0, \\pm 4, \\pm 8, \\ldots \\}$ is infinite, while\n- $B$ is finite with $\\mid B \\mid = 10$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A & \\neq & B\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude the following:\n\n> Since $A$ is infinite and $B$ is finite, we have that $\\begin{vmatrix}A & \\neq & B\\end{vmatrix}$." 13194139534215 17592186045417 false] [17592186171718 1074 "The cardinalities of two sets cannot be equal if one set is finite and the other is infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, notice that \n\n- $A=\\{ 0, \\pm 4, \\pm 8, \\ldots \\}$ is infinite, while\n\n- $B$ is finite with  $|B| = 10.$\n\nTherefore, $|A| \\neq |B|.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude the following:\n\nSince $A$ is infinite and $B$ is finite, we have that $|A| \\neq |B|$." 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171719 1071 "Do the following sets have the same cardinality? If so, why?\n\n$$\nA= \\{ -7, -2, 3, 8, \\ldots  \\}, \\qquad B = \\mathbb{N}\n$$" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171719 1071 "Do the following sets have the same cardinality? If so, why?\n\n$A= \\{-7, -2, 3, 8, \\ldots \\}, \\qquad B = \\mathbb{N}$" 13194139534215 17592186045417 false] [17592186171719 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B$.\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= 5 \\\\\n&=-2 - (-7) \\\\\n&= 3 - (-2) \\\\\n&= ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= -7 + (n - 1) \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&=-7 + 5n - 5 \\\\\n&= 5n - 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{5n-12 \\mid \\: n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: B \\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\cdots$ | $n$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $A$ | $-7$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $8$ | $\\cdots$ | $5n-12$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n \\in B$, we have\n\n$$\nf(n) = 5n-12 \\in A\n$$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n) \\neq f(m)$ whenever $n \\neq m$, and\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A & = & B\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality." 13194139534215 17592186045417 false] [17592186171719 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5 \\\\  & =-2-(-7) \\\\  & =3-(-2) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-7+(n-1)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-7+5n-5 \\\\  & =5n-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{5n-12 \\mid \\:  n \\in \\mathbb{N}  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: B \\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\cdots$ | $n$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $-7$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $8$ | $\\cdots$ | $5n-12$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n \\in B,$ we have $f(n) = 5n-12 \\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n) \\neq f(m)$ whenever $n \\neq m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A| = |B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171744 1071 "Do the following sets have the same cardinality? If so, why?\n\n$$\nA = \\mathbb{N}, \\qquad B = \\left\\{1,\\, \\dfrac12,\\,  \\dfrac14,\\,  \\dfrac18,\\,  \\ldots \\right \\}\n$$" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171744 1071 "Do the following sets have the same cardinality? If so, why?\n$A = \\mathbb{N}, \\qquad B = \\left\\{1,\\, \\dfrac12,\\, \\dfrac14,\\, \\dfrac18,\\, \\ldots \\right \\}$" 13194139534215 17592186045417 false] [17592186171744 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B$.\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio\n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac12{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{1}{2}))/(1) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{1}{4}))/((\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\n&= ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 1 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{2})^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as\n\n$$\nB = \\left\\{\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\, \\Bigg \\mid \\: n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\cdots$ | $n$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $B$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1}$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n \\in A$, we have\n\n$$\nf(n) = \\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\in B\n$$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n) \\neq f(m)$ whenever $n \\neq m$, and\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A & = & B\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality." 13194139534215 17592186045417 false] [17592186171744 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=\\dfrac12{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =1\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB = \\left\\{  \\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\,  \\Bigg |\\:  n \\in \\mathbb{N}  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\cdots$ | $n$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1}$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n \\in A,$ we have $f(n) =   \\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n) \\neq f(m)$ whenever $n \\neq m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A| = |B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414416 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414417 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414420 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414421 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414426 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414418 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414419 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414423 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414425 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414427 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414422 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414424 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414428 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186171921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414429 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186172199 1050 420.0 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186172390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169208 13194139534215 17592186045417 false] [17592186172390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169214 13194139534215 17592186045417 false] [17592186172390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169218 13194139534215 17592186045417 false] [17592186172390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169221 13194139534215 17592186045417 false] [17592186172390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414375 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186172390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414397 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186172390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414399 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186172390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414400 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186172390 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186172390 1071 "$$\nA={3,6,12,24,…},\\,B=N\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ (shown above):" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186172390 1071 "$A = {3, 6, 12, 24, …}, B = N$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ (shown above):\n\nSince the function $f: B → A$, defined as\n$3 \\cdot 2^{n - 1}$ {{field-1}}\nis both {{field-2}} and {{field-3}}, we have that {{field-4}}." 13194139534215 17592186045417 false] [17592186172390 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nA={3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}∣\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:B\\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $3$ | $6$ | $12$ | $24$ | $⋯$ | $3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in B,$ we have $f(n)=3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}\\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186173263 1064 "We recently defined two *finite* sets $A$ and $B$ as having the *same cardinality* if they contain the same number of elements.\n\nWhile this might seem a reasonable definition, we'd like to extend the concept of cardinality to infinite sets. Unfortunately, this definition will not do since counting the elements of an infinite set is impossible.\n\nTo get around this difficulty, we need a revised definition. Here it is:\n\n> *Two set $A$ and $B$ have the **same cardinality** if there exists a bijection of $A$ onto $B$. In other words, there is a one-to-one correspondence between the elements of the sets. Otherwise, if no such bijection exists, the sets are said to have **unequal cardinalities**.*\n\nAs before, we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}A &= & B\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhen $A$ and $B$ have equal cardinalities, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}A & \\neq & B\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\notherwise.\n\nFor finite sets, the situation is pretty simple. If two sets $A$ and $B$ have equal cardinality, then there exists a mapping that uniquely pairs each element of $A$ with an element of $B$. This is equivalent to saying that $A$ and $B$ contain the same number of elements.\n\nFor example, the sets\n\n$$\nA = \\{a,b,c, d \\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nB = \\{0, 1, 2, 3 \\}\n$$\n\neach have a cardinality of $4$. The corresponding bijection might be the following:\n\n![](images/d8/d8d2c9de97d811be0e38ec1512546a399ebc3ee299934746a8d47bf30354b7b1.png)\n\nIn contrast, for the sets\n\n$$\nC = \\{\\triangle \\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD = \\{\\clubsuit, \\spadesuit \\}\n$$\n\nno bijections exist between these sets since they have different numbers of elements. Therefore, they have unequal cardinalities.\n\nFor infinite sets, it's a bit more complicated. However, we have the following fact to get us started:\n\n> *If a set $A$ is finite and another set $B$ is infinite, then these sets have unequal cardinalities.*" 13194139534215 17592186045417 false] [17592186173263 1064 "We recently defined two *finite* sets $A$ and $B$ as having the *same cardinality* if they contain the same number of elements. \n\nWhile this might seem a reasonable definition, we'd like to extend the concept of cardinality to infinite sets. Unfortunately, this definition will not do since counting the elements of an infinite set is impossible. \n\nTo get around this difficulty, we need a revised definition. Here it is:\n\n*Two set $A$ and $B$ have the **same cardinality** if there exists a bijection of $A$ onto $B.$ In other words, there is a one-to-one correspondence between the elements of the sets. Otherwise, if no such bijection exists, the sets are said to have **unequal cardinalities**.*\n\nAs before, we write $|A|= |B|$ when $A$ and $B$ have equal cardinalities, and $|A|\\neq |B|$ otherwise.\n\nFor finite sets, the situation is pretty simple. If two sets $A$ and $B$ have equal cardinality, then there exists a mapping that uniquely pairs each element of $A$ with an element of $B.$ This is equivalent to saying that $A$ and $B$ contain the same number of elements. \n\nFor example, the sets $A = \\{ a,b,c, d \\}$ and $B = \\{ 0, 1, 2, 3 \\}$ each have a cardinality of $4.$ The corresponding bijection might be the following:\n\n![](images/d8/d8d2c9de97d811be0e38ec1512546a399ebc3ee299934746a8d47bf30354b7b1.png)\n\n   \n\nIn contrast, for the sets $C = \\{ \\triangle \\}$ and $D = \\{ \\clubsuit, \\spadesuit \\},$ no bijections exist between these sets since they have different numbers of elements. Therefore, they have unequal cardinalities.\n\nFor infinite sets, it's a bit more complicated. However, we have the following fact to get us started:\n\n*If a set $A$ is finite and another set $B$ is infinite, then these sets have unequal cardinalities.*" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414228 1080 "differentiable" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414228 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414228 1083 #uuid "01b4ed91-d3e3-58d2-abd5-d087fd64c99c" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [1759