e sets have the same cardinality if and only if they have the same number of elements.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $X$ is a finite set and $|X|=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $Y={1,2,3,4}$ is a finite set and $|Y|=4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $X$ and $Y$ are finite sets and  $|X|=|Y|=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414373 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414385 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414415 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414418 1069 "q-181681" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414418 1071 "$$\nA={2,0,-2,-4,…},\\,B=N\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ (shown above):" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414418 1072 #uuid "1e8f5aa7-7ccd-5ed3-b543-c8ead40f433d" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414418 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =-2 \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2-0 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2+(n-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2-2n+2 \\\\  & =-2n+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nA={-2n+4∣\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:B\\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-4$ | $⋯$ | $-2n+4$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in B,$ we have $f(n)=-2n+4\\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414376 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414404 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414405 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414419 1069 "q-216534" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414419 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414419 1071 "$$\nA=N,\\,B={3,1,-1,-3,…}\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ above:" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414419 1072 #uuid "2a225633-5b65-55d6-85ca-1c31e57a48fb" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414419 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =-2 \\\\  & =1-3 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${b}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =3+(n-1)(-2) \\\\  & =3-2n+2 \\\\  & =5-2n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB={5-2n∣\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:A\\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $⋯$ | $5-2n$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in A,$ we have $f(n)=5-2n\\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414379 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414410 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414411 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414420 1069 "q-182647" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414420 1071 "$$\nU={5n-4∣\\,n\\in Z},\\,V={4,8,…,40}\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $W$ and $V$ above:" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414420 1072 #uuid "31706254-2d24-50b4-a836-dd024a83aac7" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414420 1074 "The cardinalities of two sets cannot be equal if one set is finite and the other is infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, notice that \n\n- $U={…,-14,-9,-4,1,6,…}$ is infinite, while\n\n- $V$ is finite where $|V|=10.$\n\nTherefore, $|U|\\ne |V|.$" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414380 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414421 1069 "q-181626" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414421 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414421 1071 "$$\nS={1,1,2,3,{4,5}},\\,T={n\\in N∣\\,2\\le n\\le 7}\n$$\n\nConsider the sets $S$ and $T$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $|S|=4$\n- $|T|=5$\n- $|S|=|T|$" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414421 1072 #uuid "4c2743b2-72ab-5576-b421-aa5dcedd6c11" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414421 1074 "Recall that finite sets have the same cardinality if and only if they have the same number of elements.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,  $S$ is a finite set and $|S|=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $T={2,3,4,5,6,7}$ is a finite set and $|T|=6.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $4\\ne 6,$ we conclude that $|S|\\ne |T|.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is I only.\"" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414382 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414390 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414403 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414409 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414422 1069 "q-181750" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414422 1071 "$$\nA=N,\\,B={2,10,50,250,…}\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ (shown above):" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414422 1072 #uuid "7c2c4f15-f70f-5bf2-aa53-6e682a54a3eb" 13194139534215 17592186045417 true] [17592186414422 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =5 \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{10} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2\\cdot {5}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB={2\\cdot {5}^{n-1}∣\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:A\\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $2$ | $10$ | $50$ | $250$ | $⋯$ | $2\\cdot {5}^{n-1}$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in A,$ we have $f(n)=2\\cdot {5}^{n-1}\\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that 